Abstract

Stroke is one of the leading causes of death and long-term disability across the world. Early prediction of stroke risk enables healthcare professionals to take preventive measures and improve patient care. This project develops multiple Machine Learning classification models to predict whether a patient is likely to suffer from a stroke based on medical and demographic information. Five classification algorithms were implemented and compared using standard performance metrics. A Streamlit web application was also developed to allow users to upload test data, select a trained model, and obtain prediction results.

1. Introduction

Machine Learning has become an important tool in healthcare because it helps predict diseases from historical patient data. Classification algorithms are commonly used to determine whether a patient belongs to a particular disease category.

In this project, different supervised machine learning algorithms were trained and evaluated for stroke prediction. Their performance was compared using several evaluation metrics to identify the best-performing model.

2. Problem Statement

Develop a Machine Learning classification system capable of predicting whether a patient is likely to experience a stroke based on various demographic and medical attributes.

The project also includes a Streamlit web application that enables users to upload a CSV file, select a machine learning model, and generate prediction results.

